<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">1990 People's Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1990</span> · Also known as Jana Andolan, First Jana Andolan, 1990 Nepalese revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1990 People's Movement, or Jana Andolan, was a campaign of mass protests and strikes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from February to April 1990, led by the banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepali-congress">Nepali Congress</a> and an alliance of leftist parties. It forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> to lift the ban on political parties and end the partyless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panchayat">Panchayat</a> system. A new constitution in November 1990 made Nepal a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with a multiparty parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1990-peoples-movement.jpg" alt="1990 People's Movement" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Min Ratna Bajracharya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Iconic_Photograph_of_1990_Nepalese_revolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
